(   [Problem Statement]: Compare HTML5 <canvas> and Scalable Vector Graphics \(<svg>\), detailing their architectures, advantages, and use cases.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   HTML5 provides two distinct paradigms for rendering graphical illustrations in web pages:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. HTML5 <canvas> \(Raster / Immediate Mode\):) Tj
T*
(   - Script-driven bitmap surface accessed via JavaScript 2D or WebGL 3D context.) Tj
T*
(   - Resolution-dependent: Graphics are rendered into pixels; scaling causes pixelation.) Tj
T*
(   - Stateless: The browser does not retain drawn objects in memory. If a shape moves, the entire) Tj
T*
(   canvas must be cleared and redrawn.) Tj
T*
(   - Best suited for: High-speed intensive animations, video manipulation, physics engines, and) Tj
T*
(   online games with thousands of moving sprites.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. SVG \(<svg>\) \(Vector / Retained Mode\):) Tj
T*
(   - XML-based vector graphics language where every shape \(<circle>, <path>, <rect>\) is an active) Tj
T*
(   DOM node.) Tj
T*
(   - Resolution-independent: Crisp scaling at any resolution, zoom level, or high-DPI Retina) Tj
T*
(   screen.) Tj
T*
(   - Interactive: Individual shapes support CSS hover styling, animations, and JavaScript DOM) Tj
T*
(   event listeners \(click, touch\).) Tj
T*
(   - Best suited for: Logos, UI icons, responsive diagrams, charts, and interactive maps.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
