(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the HTML5 Content Security Policy \(CSP\) meta tag and its role in mitigating Cross-Site Scripting \(XSS\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Content Security Policy \(CSP\) is a browser security mechanism that restricts the resources) Tj
T*
(   \(scripts, images, stylesheets, fonts\) that a page is allowed to load and execute:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Declaring via Meta Tag:) Tj
T*
(   `<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="default-src 'self'; script-src 'self') Tj
T*
(   https://trustedscripts.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline';">`) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Defensive Mechanisms against XSS:) Tj
T*
(   1. Disabling Inline Scripts: By default, CSP blocks inline `<script>` tags and inline event) Tj
T*
(   handlers \(`onclick="..."`\), preventing injected attacker payloads from running.) Tj
T*
(   2. Whitelisting Domains: Scripts can only load from explicitly whitelisted domains.) Tj
T*
(   3. Disabling `eval\(\)`: Eliminates code execution from dynamic strings, shutting down a primary) Tj
T*
(   vector for DOM-based XSS attacks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
